<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hong Taiji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1592">1592</a>–<a href="/years/1643">1643</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hong Taiji was the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> as leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>. He renamed his people Manchu and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in 1636, while extending Manchu power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. His reign prepared the ground for the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the foundation of the Qing Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hong-taiji.jpg" alt="Hong Taiji" width="285" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Qing Dynasty Court Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B8%85%E4%BA%BA%E7%94%BB%E7%9A%87%E5%A4%AA%E6%9E%81%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F%E8%BD%B4_2418_x_3564.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hong Taiji was born on 28 November 1592, the eighth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>. It is unclear whether Hong Taiji was a title or a personal name: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> form was borrowed from the Mongolian title khong tayiji, itself derived from the Chinese huang taizi, crown prince. The modern Chinese form Huang Taiji wrongly implies that he was heir apparent, since Nurhaci never named a successor. At Nurhaci's death there were four senior beiles, and Hong Taiji held the lowest rank among them but was regarded as the most fit to rule. After the second son Daisan gave his endorsement, the other beiles urged him to take the throne, and he succeeded as the second ruler of the Later Jin in 1626.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Hong Taiji began recruiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> officials and in 1632 he and Prince Yoto arranged a mass marriage of 1,000 Han officers and officials to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> women. He developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a>, which could take in the Han and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> who joined the state by agreement or defeat. By 1636 he had created the first Chinese companies, and by his death there were more ethnic Han than Manchus in the banners. He also adopted from the Ming the Six Ministries and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/censorate">Censorate</a>, each ministry having two Manchu, one Mongol and one Han president. In 1636 he set up the Mongol Yamen, renamed Lifanyuan in 1638, to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> indirectly.</p><p>In February 1627 his forces crossed the frozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. In 1628 an attempt to invade the Ming was defeated by Yuan Chonghuan, who used artillery, and Hong Taiji spent the next five years training his own artillery. Raids into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1632 and 1634 were defeated. In 1635 he changed the name of his people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>, whose original meaning is unknown, and in 1636 he changed the name of his dynasty from Great Jin to Great Qing. Korea became a Qing tributary state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hong Taiji died on 21 September 1643, as the Qing prepared to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanhai-pass">Shanhai Pass</a>, the last Ming fortification guarding the north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> plains. He had named no heir. The princes and ministers debated between his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorgon">Dorgon</a> and his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooge">Hooge</a>, and as a compromise chose his five-year-old ninth son Fulin, with Dorgon and Jirgalang as prince regents. Fulin was crowned on 8 October 1643 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a>, and a few months later Qing armies under Dorgon took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. His body was buried at Zhaoling, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a>. Some historians regard him as the true first emperor of the Qing and think he has been underrated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hong Taiji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Meanwhile the Manchus, united under Nurhaci and <b>Hong Taiji</b>, pressed on the north-east frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Hong Taiji</b> renamed the people “Manchu” in 1635, distancing them from the Jurchen Jin dynasty that had once ruled northern China, and proclaimed the Qing dynasty in 1636, presenting it as a legitimate successor within the Chinese imperial tradition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The later Qing emperor <b>Hong Taiji</b> told another legend, of a heavenly maiden who became pregnant after eating a red fruit dropped by a magpie.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jurchens">Jurchens · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Hong Taiji</b> brought in Chinese officials, subdued Korea and the Chahar Mongols, renamed his people Manchu in 1635 and in 1636 proclaimed the Qing Empire, which conquered China from 1644.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-jin-1616-1636">Later Jin (1616-1636)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1635 his son Ejei surrendered with the Yuan imperial seal to <b>Hong Taiji</b> of the Later Jin (1616-1636), who soon founded the Qing Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a></li><li><blockquote>Ligdan Khan of the Chahar overran their lands and Guihua in 1627 to 1628; when <b>Hong Taiji's</b> campaign of 1632 drove him west, the Tümed soon submitted to the Later Jin (1616-1636).</blockquote><a href="/state/tumed-altan-khan">Tumed (Altan Khan)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetu-ala">Hetu Ala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.7,124.86&amp;year=1625" aria-label="Hetu Ala City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hong Taiji"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5310 4000h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5310 4000h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.7,124.86&amp;year=1625">What was here in 1625? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
